---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
// qa1018 C04: ONE install phrase, shown == copied, from the canonical module.
import { META_INSTALL_PHRASE } from '../../lib/agentInstall';
---
<AppShell mode="public" title="Getting started — LoopSkill Docs"
     description="Install LoopSkill on Hermes, Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex, or Cursor in under 5 minutes.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Documentation · Getting started</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">From zero to skill-aware in 60 seconds.</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <div class="not-prose mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-accent/40 bg-accent/5 p-5">
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-accent font-semibold mb-2">The fastest way</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Paste this into your agent's chat (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor…) and press Enter:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-stretch gap-2">
          <code class="flex-1 block font-mono text-sm bg-[#0d0d0d] border border-border rounded px-3 py-2.5 text-text break-words wrap-cmd" data-phrase>{META_INSTALL_PHRASE}</code>
          <button type="button" class="phrase-copy-btn shrink-0 min-h-[44px] px-4 rounded-lg border border-border text-sm font-semibold text-text hover:border-accent transition" data-copy={META_INSTALL_PHRASE} aria-label="Copy the install phrase">Copy</button>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          Your agent reads the LoopSkill skill and can then search the catalog and install skills for you.
          Free skills need no account and no key.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>One of the supported agent runtimes below.</li>
        <li><strong>For free skills: nothing else.</strong> No account and no key — they install anonymously.</li>
        <li><strong>An API key only if you need it</strong> — for the MCP tools (the Claude Desktop and Cursor setups below), saving to your Library, private bundles and fleets. The key is free, no card required. Every catalog skill installs on the Free tier; Pro ($9.95/mo) raises two limits: private bundles and API keys.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Get a key: <a href="/signup">create a free account</a> → Library → API keys → New key. Then export it: <code>export LOOPSKILL_API_KEY=rec_xx...</code></p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Install the meta-skill</h2>
      <p>
        The meta-skill is a single <code>SKILL.md</code> file that teaches your agent the entire
        marketplace. Drop it into your agent's skills directory once. Your agent will then be able
        to search and install any skill on-demand.
      </p>

      <h3>Hermes (native)</h3>
      <p>Hermes auto-discovers SKILL.md files under <code>~/.hermes/skills/</code>:</p>
      <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.hermes/skills/loopskill
curl -sL https://app.loopskill.io/skill \
     -o ~/.hermes/skills/loopskill/SKILL.md</code></pre>

      <h3>Claude Code (native)</h3>
      <p>Claude Code reads skills from <code>~/.claude/skills/</code>:</p>
      <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills/loopskill
curl -sL https://app.loopskill.io/skill \
     -o ~/.claude/skills/loopskill/SKILL.md</code></pre>

      <h3>Codex CLI (native)</h3>
      <p>OpenAI Codex CLI uses the same skill directory layout:</p>
      <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.codex/skills/loopskill
curl -sL https://app.loopskill.io/skill \
     -o ~/.codex/skills/loopskill/SKILL.md</code></pre>

      <h3>Claude Desktop (MCP)</h3>
      <p>
        Claude Desktop uses the Model Context Protocol. LoopSkill is a <strong>hosted</strong> MCP
        server — no local install. Add it to your
        <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "loopskill": {
      "url": "https://app.loopskill.io/api/mcp/http/",
      "headers": { "x-api-key": "rec_xx..." }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        After saving, restart Claude Desktop. You'll see the LoopSkill tools (search, install, sync)
        available in the tool picker. See <a href="/docs/mcp">MCP setup</a> for full details.
      </p>

      <h3>Cursor (MCP)</h3>
      <p>Open Cursor Settings → MCP → Add Server:</p>
      <pre><code>{`{
  "name": "loopskill",
  "url": "https://app.loopskill.io/api/mcp/http/",
  "headers": { "x-api-key": "rec_xx..." }
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Step 2 — Search the catalog</h2>
      <p>
        Once the meta-skill is installed, your agent can search the catalog. Try asking:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>"Search LoopSkill for an SEO audit skill"</li>
        <li>"What LoopSkill skills exist for client reporting?"</li>
        <li>"Find a LoopSkill skill that generates content calendars"</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Under the hood, the agent calls <code>GET /api/skills/search?q=...</code> and ranks results
        by <code>quality_score</code>, <code>last_verified</code>, and install count.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Install your first skill</h2>
      <p>
        Tell your agent to install a skill. Example:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>"Install the <code>super-memory</code> skill from LoopSkill"</li>
        <li>"Install the best SEO audit skill from LoopSkill"</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The agent calls <code>GET /api/skills/install?slug=super-memory&amp;mode=files</code>,
        downloads the tarball via an expiring signed URL, verifies its <code>checksum_sha256</code>,
        and writes the files locally.
        A <code>_meta.json</code> records the skill's slug, version and install time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The free skill <strong>super-memory</strong> (Cognee + Postgres + pgvector, MIT-licensed)
        is a great first install — it works immediately with no subscription.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Stay current</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>One skill:</strong> re-run the Step 3 install with the same slug; you get the
        latest version and its sha256.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Several skills, or several machines:</strong> put them in a bundle once
        (<code>loopskill_compose_bundle_from_links</code> with your skill links — it returns the
        bundle id), then run <code>loopskill_bundle_install</code> with that id on each machine.
        To update, re-run <code>loopskill_bundle_install</code> on each machine: a bundle does not
        pin versions unless you pin it, so the install always returns the latest version of every
        skill — a download URL and sha256 each; your agent downloads each tarball, checks its
        sha256, and unpacks it into the skills folder. <code>loopskill_sync</code> is only for
        bundles you want pinned: it writes the current latest versions as the bundle's pins (it
        does not touch files on your machine), and the bundle then stays on those versions until
        you sync again.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cli">Optional — the <code>loopskill</code> command-line tool</h2>
      <p>
        A small, free command-line tool (Python 3.9+, MIT, no dependencies) that shows which skills
        are already on a machine and how two machines differ. You do not need it to install skills.
      </p>
      <pre><code>pip install loopskill</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>loopskill import</code> lists the skills in <code>~/.claude/skills</code>,
        <code>~/.codex/skills</code>, <code>~/.cursor/skills</code> and <code>~/.hermes/skills</code> as a
        lockfile, and <code>loopskill diff</code> compares two lockfiles (or a lockfile and this machine).
        Both make zero network calls and need no account.
      </p>
      <pre><code>loopskill import -o mine.lock.json
loopskill diff mine.lock.json</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>loopskill pull</code> and <code>loopskill apply</code> fetch a public bundle and write it to a
        local folder (<code>apply</code> is a dry run until you add <code>--write</code>). Run
        <code>loopskill --help</code> for every command. Source and full reference:
        <a href="https://github.com/wisechef-ai/loopskill-api/tree/main/cli">cli/ on GitHub</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What's in the catalog</h2>
      <p>
        Skills across marketing, code, web scraping, ops, and sales — see the live count on
        <a href="/skills">the catalog page</a> or <code>GET /api/stats</code>. Every skill carries
        a <code>quality_score</code>, install count, and last-verified date.
      </p>
      <p>
        Browse the <a href="/skills">full catalog</a> — but the fastest path is always
        to ask your agent.
      </p>

      <h2>Pricing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Free:</strong> 1 API key (it works on all your machines at once), catalog access, all skills (including super-memory), 2 private bundles (your Liked list uses one from the moment you open your Library).</li>
        <li><strong>Pro ($9.95/mo):</strong> 50 private bundles, 10 API keys (revoke one machine or client without touching the rest), priority support. The full public catalog is already unlimited on Free — Pro raises limits, it doesn't unlock exclusive skills. Public bundles are unlimited on every tier, including Free.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-host:</strong> run the whole platform yourself (MPL-2.0) — no LoopSkill account, no card; the caps live in config/tiers.yaml on your server, so you can raise them. See the <a href="https://github.com/wisechef-ai/loopskill-api/blob/main/docs/SELF_HOST.md">self-host guide</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Next steps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/how-it-works">How it works</a> — the 3-level progressive loading model</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/mcp">MCP setup</a> — full Claude Desktop / Cursor / Cline config</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/new-agent">New agent runtime</a> — 10-minute guide for unlisted runtimes</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/security">Security model</a> — auth, install checksums, sandboxing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppShell>

<script>
  // Copy button for the install phrase: copies exactly the text shown.
  import { writeClipboard } from '../../lib/agentInstall';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.phrase-copy-btn').forEach((btn) => {
    btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const text = btn.getAttribute('data-copy') || '';
      const how = await writeClipboard(text);
      if (how === 'manual') {
        const code = btn.parentElement?.querySelector('[data-phrase]');
        if (code) { const r = document.createRange(); r.selectNodeContents(code); const sel = window.getSelection(); sel?.removeAllRanges(); sel?.addRange(r); }
        btn.textContent = 'Select & copy';
        return;
      }
      btn.textContent = 'Copied ✓';
      setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1600);
    });
  });
</script>
